g owned requirements, contradictions, access/budget limits and highest-impact resolving check.\"},\"stopReason\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"minLength\":1,\"description\":\"Actual sufficient-support, diminishing-return, access, budget or blocked-conclusion reason. Budget stops are not evidence saturation.\"}}}}","status":"failed","runner":"pi","kernel":"typescript","transport":"process","cwd":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/.worktrees/research-skill-rework","model":"openai-codex/gpt-5.6-terra","thinking":"high","startedAt":1788761220562,"updatedAt":1788761340407,"turns":7,"toolCalls":6,"text":"{\"assignment\":\"concurrency\",\"discovered\":[\"extensions.web_search\",\"extensions.fetch_content\",\"extensions.get_search_content\"],\"calls\":[{\"ref\":\"extensions.web_search\",\"args\":{\"queries\":[\"site:postgresql.org/docs/current MVCC concurrent update locking transaction isolation\",\"site:postgresql.org/docs/current client authentication TCP IP connections local Unix-domain socket\"],\"numResults\":5,\"workflow\":\"none\"},\"retrieved\":true,\"detail\":\"2/2 queries successful; 10 total results.\",\"responseId\":\"mtqu9h6urg76zb\"},{\"ref\":\"extensions.fetch_content\",\"args\":{\"urls\":[\"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html\",\"https://www.sqlite.org/isolation.html\",\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html\",\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html\",\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/protocol.html\"],\"mode\":\"readable\"},\"retrieved\":true,\"detail\":\"5/5 URLs fetched; 73,483 total extracted characters.\",\"responseId\":\"mtqu9mjzeqh0sh\"},{\"ref\":\"extensions.get_search_content\",\"args\":{\"responseId\":\"mtqu9mjzeqh0sh\",\"urlIndex\":0,\"findText\":[\"readers do not block writers\",\"only one writer at a time\",\"network filesystem\"],\"findMode\":\"case-insensitive\"},\"retrieved\":true,\"detail\":\"4 matches returned; the literal query \\\"only one writer at a time\\\" did not match, but the returned WAL passage states that there can only be one writer at a time.\",\"responseId\":\"mtqu9mjzeqh0sh\"},{\"ref\":\"extensions.get_search_content\",\"args\":{\"responseId\":\"mtqu9mjzeqh0sh\",\"urlIndex\":3,\"findText\":[\"Read Committed is the default isolation level\",\"two transactions concurrently update the same row\",\"will wait for the first updating transaction to commit or roll back\",\"applications using this level must be prepared to retry transactions\"],\"findMode\":\"case-insensitive\"},\"retrieved\":true,\"detail\":\"4 matches returned; the two exact default-isolation/concurrent-row query strings did not match, while wait and retry passages did.\",\"responseId\":\"mtqu9mjzeqh0sh\"},{\"ref\":\"extensions.get_search_content\",\"args\":{\"responseId\":\"mtqu9mjzeqh0sh\",\"urlIndex\":4,\"offset\":0,\"limit\":3000},\"retrieved\":true,\"detail\":\"Complete 997-character PostgreSQL protocol introduction returned.\",\"responseId\":\"mtqu9mjzeqh0sh\"}],\"rows\":[{\"id\":\"sqlite-wal-concurrency\",\"slot\":\"concurrency\",\"finding\":\"SQLite WAL documents reader-writer overlap, but limits the database to one WAL writer at a time.\",\"status\":\"documented_fact\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html\",\"title\":\"Write-Ahead Logging\",\"passage\":\"WAL provides more concurrency as readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers. Reading and writing can proceed concurrently.\",\"locator\":\"Advantages and Disadvantages\"},{\"url\":\"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html\",\"title\":\"Write-Ahead Logging\",\"passage\":\"Because writers do nothing that would interfere with the actions of readers, writers and readers can run at the same time. However, since there is only one WAL file, there can only be one writer at a time.\",\"locator\":\"How WAL Works\",\"origin\":\"SQLite official documentation\",\"sourceType\":\"Primary documentation\"}],\"counterevidence\":\"The same page says reader/writer nonblocking is \\\"mostly true\\\" and identifies obscure cases in which a WAL-mode query can return SQLITE_BUSY.\",\"method\":\"Applies only when SQLite is operating in WAL mode; this is documented locking behavior, not a throughput measurement.\",\"comparability\":\"SQLite's single-WAL-writer constraint is not directly comparable to PostgreSQL without workload, transaction duration, isolation, storage, and contention measurements.\",\"confidence\":\"High for the stated WAL behavior because both conditions are direct official documentation.\",\"disposition\":\"retain\"},{\"id\":\"postgresql-conflicting-writers\",\"slot\":\"concurrency\",\"finding\":\"PostgreSQL documents that a would-be updater encountering a concurrently updated, deleted, or locked target row waits for the first updater; at Repeatable Read, a committed competing update can roll back the transaction and require retry.\",\"status\":\"documented_fact\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html\",\"title\":\"13.2. Transaction Isolation\",\"passage\":\"However, such a target row might have already been updated (or deleted or locked) by another concurrent transaction by the time it is found. In this case, the would-be updater will wait for the first updating transaction to commit or roll back (if it is still in progress).\",\"locator\":\"13.2.1 Read Committed Isolation Level\",\"origin\":\"PostgreSQL official documentation\",\"sourceType\":\"Primary documentation\"},{\"url\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html\",\"title\":\"13.2. Transaction Isolation\",\"passage\":\"Applications using this level must be prepared to retry transactions due to serialization failures.\",\"locator\":\"13.2.2 Repeatable Read Isolation Level\",\"origin\":\"PostgreSQL official documentation\",\"sourceType\":\"Primary documentation\"}],\"counterevidence\":\"The retry statement is specific to Repeatable Read; the first passage describes the would-be updater behavior and does not establish a throughput outcome.\",\"method\":\"Applies to the documented transaction isolation semantics and conflicts on a target row; no load test or throughput measurement was retrieved.\",\"comparability\":\"Isolation level materially changes conflict handling. These passages cannot establish comparative writer throughput against SQLite WAL.\",\"confidence\":\"High for the conflict and retry semantics within the named isolation contexts; limited for general reader-writer overlap because no exact MVCC passage was expanded.\",\"disposition\":\"qualify\"},{\"id\":\"deployment-topology\",\"slot\":\"deployment\",\"finding\":\"SQLite WAL requires all database processes on one host and does not work over a network filesystem; PostgreSQL's client-server protocol supports TCP/IP and Unix-domain sockets.\",\"status\":\"documented_fact\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html\",\"title\":\"Write-Ahead Logging\",\"passage\":\"All processes using a database must be on the same host computer; WAL does not work over a network filesystem. This is because WAL requires all processes to share a small amount of memory and processes on separate host machines obviously cannot share memory with each other.\",\"locator\":\"Advantages and Disadvantages\",\"origin\":\"SQLite official documentation\",\"sourceType\":\"Primary documentation\"},{\"url\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/protocol.html\",\"title\":\"Chapter 54. Frontend/Backend Protocol\",\"passage\":\"PostgreSQL uses a message-based protocol for communication between frontends and backends (clients and servers). The protocol is supported over TCP/IP and also over Unix-domain sockets.\",\"locator\":\"Chapter introduction; protocol version 3.2 introduced in PostgreSQL 18\",\"origin\":\"PostgreSQL official documentation\",\"sourceType\":\"Primary documentation\"}],\"method\":\"Documents supported connection and filesystem topology, not deployment effort, backup configuration, security posture, or performance.\",\"comparability\":\"SQLite